<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Trarza</h1><p class="st-meta">1640 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The emirate of south-western Mauritania whose rulers controlled the gum trade with Europeans and resisted French expansion on the Senegal River.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Trarza <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> took shape in the 17th century among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hassan">Hassani Arab</a> groups of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, ruled by the Awlad Ahmed min <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daman">Daman</a> lineage. It consolidated after the Char Bouba war of the 1670s, in which Hassani warriors defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-din-mauritanian-cleric">Nasir al-Din</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ruled a nomadic society of warriors, scholarly tribes, tributaries and enslaved labourers. Gum arabic, sold to the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a> posts and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portendick">Portendick</a>, was their main source of wealth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-shandora">Ali Shandora</a> in the early 18th century made Trarza a regional power, and the emirs dominated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/waalo">Waalo</a> kingdom.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-habib-of-trarza">Muhammad al-Habib</a> fought the French governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Faidherbe</a> in the 1850s, losing control over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/waalo">Waalo</a>. Trarza accepted a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1902 to 1903, and was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-trarza.jpg" alt="Emirate of Trarza" width="320" height="170"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emirate_of_Trarza_Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1640 CE – 1902 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Shandora, Muhammad al-Habib</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Trarza</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1771&amp;focus=emirate-of-trarza" aria-label="Emirate of Trarza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4260 1528 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Trarza lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4580 1784L4567 1763"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4567 1763h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4580 1784h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4580 1784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1771&amp;focus=emirate-of-trarza">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

